diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the Kath2 topology finds no network-layer fault. Every CS-lab machine (cs_hod through cs_lab4, all on 192.168.2.0/24) can reach the Server Room host pc1 (1.0.0.5/8) with zero packet loss in both directions. TCP probes from cs_lab1 to pc1 receive Connection Refused responses for ports 80, 445, and 22 — "Connection Refused" is the server's own TCP RST reply, which is proof-positive that packets traverse the full path (CS LAN → switch1 → router0 → WAN r0-r1 → router1 → WAN r1-r2 → router2 → switch4 → pc1) and that pc1 is responding. A timeout or unreachable error would indicate a network drop; Connection Refused does not.

RIP v2 is fully converged on all three routers. Router0 holds 1.0.0.0/8 at metric 3, router1 at metric 2, and router2 shows it as directly connected — all exactly correct. The reverse path (192.168.2.0/24) is identically correct on every router. No distribute-lists, offset-lists, or route filters exist. All interface addresses and default gateways on end hosts match the topology design. No iptables or nftables rules are present on any router or server. IP forwarding is enabled on all routers and L2 forwarding on switch4 is healthy.

The root cause of the CS users' complaint is not a network problem: the IP routing infrastructure is fully functional. The reported inability to access file shares is most likely caused by the application or service layer on pc1 (no file-sharing daemon is listening), not by a routing or switching fault. No corrective network action is required; the investigation should be redirected to application services running on the server host.
=== END ===